@* Section IV — Law for Professional Practice *@
<p>
    This section gives you the legal foundation professionals work within — worth about <strong>20%
    (23–28 questions)</strong>. You don’t need a law degree, but you do need to recognize the core concepts
    of contracts, torts, business and employment law, dispute resolution, and intellectual property.
</p>

<h2 id="legal-system"><span class="rref">IV.1</span> The Canadian Legal System</h2>
<p>
    The Canadian Constitution combines British and Canadian parliamentary components and rests on three
    elements: the division of powers, the creation of the courts, and the Charter of Rights and Freedoms.
    Canada has two kinds of law: <strong>statute law</strong> (enacted by the federal and provincial
    governments) and <strong>common law</strong> (judge-made law, based on previous rulings).
</p>
<div class="callout">
    <span class="clabel">Theory of precedent</span>
    <p>
        Also called <em>stare decisis</em> (“to stand by things decided”), this principle requires courts to
        follow the rulings of earlier cases when deciding cases with similar facts.
    </p>
</div>

<h2 id="contract"><span class="rref">IV.2</span> Contract Law — Elements, Principles &amp; Applications</h2>
<p>Whether oral or written, a valid contract needs five elements:</p>
<ol>
    <li><strong>Offer</strong> — a promise made under certain terms</li>
    <li><strong>Mutual acceptance</strong> — both parties accept and intend to be bound</li>
    <li><strong>Consideration</strong> — an exchange of things of value</li>
    <li><strong>Capacity</strong> — the parties are of legal age and sound mind</li>
    <li><strong>Lawful purpose</strong> — the contract isn’t for anything illegal</li>
</ol>
<p>
    A <strong>Letter of Intent (LOI)</strong> records a preliminary understanding but is usually
    <em>not</em> binding, because it lacks all the elements of a contract. Courts void contracts only in
    limited cases: against public policy, severely one-sided/unconscionable, illegal, agreed by incompetent
    parties (e.g. minors), impossible to perform, restricting fundamental rights, or tainted by mistake,
    fraud, or duress.
</p>
<h3>Key doctrines</h3>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Fraudulent misrepresentation</strong> requires: a false representation, knowledge of (or recklessness about) its falsehood, intent to be relied upon, actual reliance, and harm suffered.</li>
    <li><strong>Force majeure</strong> (“superior force”) — a clause covering events beyond the parties’ control that prevent performance.</li>
    <li><strong>Estoppel</strong> — a party that acts contrary to its earlier promise may lose the right to enforce the original term.</li>
    <li><strong>Quantum meruit</strong> — no one should pay, or receive, more than the value of the services exchanged; often the market value, even absent a formal contract.</li>
    <li><strong>Parole evidence rule</strong> — extrinsic evidence can’t modify a written contract; make amendments in writing.</li>
    <li><strong>Indemnity</strong> (hold-harmless) — one party agrees to bear another’s loss, like an insurance policy.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Breach &amp; remedies</h3>
<p>The four types of breach are <strong>material</strong>, <strong>minor</strong>, <strong>anticipatory</strong>, and <strong>actual</strong>. The usual remedy is money (“damages”); less common remedies are specific performance, injunction, and declaratory order. Damages are governed by three principles: <strong>mitigation</strong> (minimize your loss), <strong>no speculation</strong> (prove the loss with reasonable certainty), and <strong>remoteness</strong> (only reasonably foreseeable losses). A contract can be discharged by performance, breach, intention, mutual agreement, lapse of time, frustration, or operation of law.</p>

<h2 id="tort"><span class="rref">IV.3</span> Tort Law — Negligence &amp; Misrepresentation</h2>
<p>
    A <strong>tort</strong> arises when someone fails their duty of care and causes injury or loss — no
    agreement required. Tort law aims to <em>compensate victims</em>, not punish. To win, a plaintiff must
    prove all four:
</p>
<ol>
    <li><strong>Duty</strong> — the defendant owed a duty of care</li>
    <li><strong>Breach</strong> — that duty was not met</li>
    <li><strong>Causation</strong> — a real, demonstrable loss caused by the defendant</li>
    <li><strong>Proximate cause</strong> — the injury was a foreseeable result</li>
</ol>
<p>
    Categories include intentional, defamation (slander is spoken, libel is written), negligence (the most
    common), nuisance (private or public), and strict-liability torts (inherently hazardous activities such
    as using explosives). Misrepresentation comes in three forms: <strong>fraudulent</strong> (a knowing
    lie), <strong>negligent</strong> (careless, without checking), and <strong>innocent</strong> (believed
    true).
</p>

<h2 id="quebec"><span class="rref">IV.4</span> Civil Law in Quebec</h2>
<p>
    Quebec is governed by the <strong>Civil Code of Québec</strong> rather than common law, covering property
    rights, obligations and contracts, family law, and civil liability. Some federal common law — criminal
    law, patent law, and certain federal statutes — still applies in Quebec.
</p>

<h2 id="business"><span class="rref">IV.5</span> Business, Employment &amp; Labour Law</h2>
<p>There are three main business forms, each with trade-offs:</p>
<div class="rtable">
    <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Form</th><th>Advantages</th><th>Disadvantages</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Sole proprietorship</strong></td>
                <td>Cheap and easy to set up; few rules; full control; all profits to owner; tax advantages</td>
                <td>Unlimited personal liability; hard to raise money; ends with the owner’s death</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Partnership</strong></td>
                <td>Cheap and easy to set up; few rules; not dependent on one person; tax advantages</td>
                <td>Partners carry unlimited debts/liabilities; divided authority; hard to raise money</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Corporation</strong></td>
                <td>Limited liability; specialized management; separate legal entity; transferable ownership; easier to raise money</td>
                <td>Strictly regulated; heavy tax filings; expensive to set up; more chance of disagreement</td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
</div>
<p>
    <strong>Employment law</strong> governs the employer–worker relationship: the employment contract,
    workplace safety, termination (unfair/wrongful dismissal), and anti-discrimination. An employee owes a
    <strong>duty of fidelity</strong> — not to compete with the employer during employment — and the employer
    owes a duty of competence, selecting capable staff and instructing them properly. <strong>Labour law</strong>
    (mostly provincial) covers the union–management and union–employee relationships. Restrictive covenants
    such as non-competes must be reasonable in scope, geography, and duration.
</p>

<h2 id="dispute"><span class="rref">IV.6</span> Dispute Resolution</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Negotiation</strong> — parties settle differences directly to reach a compromise</li>
    <li><strong>Mediation</strong> — a neutral third party helps the parties reach a consensual agreement</li>
    <li><strong>Arbitration</strong> — a neutral third party acts as judge; cheaper, quicker, and private than litigation</li>
    <li><strong>Litigation</strong> — the familiar court process, a defendant against a plaintiff before a judge (and possibly jury)</li>
    <li><strong>Mini-trials</strong> — a private settlement process with summarized cases before a panel that can settle</li>
    <li><strong>Settlement conferences</strong> — a meeting to resolve the dispute without going to trial</li>
</ul>
<p>Alternative Dispute Resolution (ADR) keeps matters out of court but is a poor fit for highly complex disputes or where enforcement is needed.</p>

<h2 id="ip"><span class="rref">IV.7</span> Intellectual Property</h2>
<p><em>This subsection is worth less than 5% of the exam.</em></p>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Patents</strong> — a government grant of the exclusive right to make, use, or sell an invention for <strong>20 years</strong>. The invention must be new, useful, and non-obvious (inventive). An application has an abstract, specification, drawings, and claims. The Patent Cooperation Treaty lets one Canadian filing reach up to 142 countries.</li>
    <li><strong>Trademarks (™)</strong> — distinguish one entity’s goods/services from others’ (word, logo, sound, or combination). Protection lasts <strong>10 years</strong>, renewable. Ordinary marks vs. certification marks (a seal of approval, e.g. ISO 9001).</li>
    <li><strong>Copyright</strong> — the exclusive right to copy, distribute, adapt, display, and perform an original work. Since December 2022, protection runs <strong>70 years</strong> after the author’s death. Registration isn’t required but establishes ownership.</li>
    <li><strong>Industrial design</strong> — protects a product’s form and features for up to <strong>10 years</strong> from registration (maintenance fee after 5 years).</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="expert"><span class="rref">IV.8</span> Expert Witness</h2>
<p>
    An expert witness gives a specialized opinion to a court or government body and must remain neutral and
    unbiased — even though a party pays them. To protect objectivity, they should be paid a
    <strong>predetermined fee</strong>, never a fee tied to the outcome.
</p>

<h2 id="liens"><span class="rref">IV.9</span> Bonds &amp; Construction Liens</h2>
<p>
    A <strong>construction (builder’s) lien</strong> is a legal claim registered against a property to secure
    payment for work done or materials supplied to improve it. Buyers typically run a lien search before
    purchase, since unpaid liens can pass to a new owner. A <strong>lien bond</strong> (a court-approved
    surety) lets a defendant discharge a lien. A lien isn’t legitimate until proven in court
    (“perfecting” it), and it must be registered within a set timeframe after the work is complete.
</p>

<h2 id="international"><span class="rref">IV.10</span> International Law</h2>
<p>International agreements and treaties outrank provincial or municipal law, but you must still follow the province’s rules and regulations.</p>

<h2 id="environmental-law"><span class="rref">IV.11</span> Environmental Law</h2>
<p>Environmental Site Assessments (ESA), per Canadian Standards Association guidelines, have three phases:</p>
<ol>
    <li><strong>Phase I — Assessment</strong> — preliminary work to identify potential contamination</li>
    <li><strong>Phase II — Investigation</strong> — in-depth study if Phase I suggests probable contamination</li>
    <li><strong>Phase III — Remediation</strong> — a plan to minimize or eliminate hazards</li>
</ol>
<p>Environmental audits are conducted to meet legal requirements and to defend against environmental allegations.</p>

<h2 id="ohs"><span class="rref">IV.12</span> Workers’ Compensation &amp; Occupational Health &amp; Safety</h2>
<p>
    The <strong>Workers’ Compensation Act</strong> is provincial <em>no-fault</em> insurance: employees are
    compensated for workplace injuries without suing, regardless of fault, while employers gain a degree of
    immunity from employee lawsuits. Every workplace must meet applicable <strong>OH&amp;S</strong>
    legislation, and providing a safe workplace is a legal obligation — violations can bring consequences
    under the Criminal Code. Employees have three rights: to <strong>know</strong> the hazards and be trained,
    to <strong>refuse</strong> unsafe work without punishment, and to <strong>participate</strong> in
    workplace-safety initiatives.
</p>

<h2 id="privacy"><span class="rref">IV.13</span> Human Rights &amp; Privacy Legislation</h2>
<p>
    Provincial human-rights laws protect against discrimination based on inherent characteristics such as
    age, race, or religion (with narrow exceptions for genuine job requirements). At the federal level,
    <strong>PIPEDA</strong> (the Personal Information Protection and Electronic Documents Act) protects
    personal information — though not information of a journalistic or artistic nature. Where there is a
    considerable risk to someone’s personal safety, disclosing personal information is acceptable.
</p>
